# 10.1 — Introduction to Technical SEO

Technical SEO concerns the technical conditions under which a website is delivered and made available to users and automated systems. It connects web engineering decisions with search accessibility without claiming control over a search engine's independent processing or ranking decisions.

## 1. Core concepts and mechanisms

A technically sound site should return appropriate responses, expose intended resources, avoid contradictory access and indexing instructions, render essential content reliably, and remain observable as it changes. The discipline spans architecture, crawling, indexability, rendering, mobile delivery, performance, structured data, diagnostics, and quality assurance.

A practical lifecycle is: design URL and delivery architecture; serve resources with valid HTTP behavior; expose intended resources through crawler controls and discovery aids; observe rendering, requests, and performance; then maintain the implementation through release and regression checks.

Keep separate availability, crawlability, renderability, indexability, search-engine selection, ranking, and presentation. A successful technical check does not guarantee indexing or visibility. Sitemap inclusion, valid structured data, successful rendering, or a performance score must never be described as a ranking guarantee.

Domain 02 explains provider-side search systems. Domain 10 focuses on publisher-controlled implementation. Domain 07 owns page-level authoring; Domain 08 owns editorial substance; Domain 09 owns semantic models; Domain 11 owns internal-link strategy; Domain 14 owns organic performance measurement.

Record the exact URL/template, device, crawler or browser, environment, date, response, tool, and inspection method. Separate standards requirements from Google-specific guidance and a tool warning from a confirmed defect.

## 2. Practical validation

Use representative URLs and templates, inspect the actual HTTP response and rendered output, document the environment and date, and retest after production deployment. A tool result is evidence about the test it performed, not a universal certification.

## 3. Limitations and ownership

Attribute search-engine-specific behavior to the engine that documents it. Distinguish technical validity from indexing selection, ranking, and search presentation. Use adjacent-domain links from the [Domain 10 index](README.md) to preserve canonical ownership.

